REMOVE-SMOKE-ACCOUNTS

Requested 2026-10-03 by the owner. The care-data purge earlier the same day kept every
account, because keeping the user is what was asked for. That left twelve accounts and
twelve households in production, of which one belongs to the owner and the rest are
leftovers from smoke-test runs — deploy/smoke-test.ps1 registers one synthetic
household every time it runs.

What goes: accounts whose address matches synthetic-smoke-<guid>@example.invalid,
their sessions, their memberships, their subscriptions and entitlements, and the
households left with no other member.

What stays: every other account, and any household that still has a member who is
staying — including one shared with a synthetic account.

The owner's address is deliberately not named here. This repository is public, and the
pattern is enough: .invalid is reserved by RFC 2606 so that it can never belong to
anyone, which is a stronger guarantee than a name comparison would be.

Proven against a real PostgreSQL database seeded to look like production — thirteen
accounts including a synthetic one sharing the owner's household — where it removed
twelve accounts, eleven households, twelve memberships and eleven sessions, left the
owner and their household intact, and refused in all three failure cases: every
account synthetic, a matched household still owning care data, and the wrong database
name.

This file is the confirmation. Remove it once the cleanup has run; the run that sees it
gone reports that there is nothing to do and passes.
